WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.805 --> 00:00:06.680
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Welcome
to the fifth episode

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.920
of the weekly Google
Cloud Platform Podcast.

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:12.531
I'm Francesc Campoy and I'm
here with my co-host, Mark.

00:00:12.531 --> 00:00:14.280
MARK MANDEL: Hey,
Francesc, how you doing?

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Pretty
good, pretty good.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.890
So today we're
going to be talking

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:20.990
with someone from Google.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:21.740
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:23.281
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Product manager that

00:00:23.281 --> 00:00:26.340
is responsible for the
welfare experience.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:27.140
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.650
Chris Sells.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.030
Very excited, as per usual
for all our interviews.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.280
I think it's going to be an
interesting podcast that's

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.801
going to sort of look
into the hows and whys we

00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:37.050
make the decisions that we do.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:38.674
And sort of give you
some opportunities

00:00:38.674 --> 00:00:41.770
to provide feedback so you can
help direct things as well.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:42.980
So that's pretty awesome.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:43.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.441
I think it's going to be
quite interesting to interview

00:00:45.441 --> 00:00:50.310
someone that has such an
interesting view of the tech

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:51.230
industry in general.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:55.090
He's been working so many cool
companies that-- his opinion,

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:56.700
by itself, is amazing.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.210
The fact that he has the
power of changing Google Cloud

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:01.740
Platform Podcast-- Google
Cloud Platform, sorry--

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:01.970
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:02.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --and
the way we use it.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:03.720
Even more interesting.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.178
MARK MANDEL: Even
more interesting.

00:01:05.178 --> 00:01:07.930
But let's talk about
cool things of the week.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.780
There's been a really
nice launch recently

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:13.435
that I think is quite
interesting and exciting.

00:01:13.435 --> 00:01:14.960
Custom machine types.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.560
Custom machine
types, what is that?

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.710
So far we've talked during one
of our first episodes actually

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:27.510
about Google Compute Engine and
how you can create instances.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:31.160
So basically those are
VMs and they have-- we

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:33.080
used to have-- we still
have them, actually--

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.566
a list of types of
machines that you can get.

00:01:35.566 --> 00:01:36.316
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:01:36.316 --> 00:01:38.720
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You
can get, for instance,

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:42.320
the standard one which
is-- what's the name again?

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:43.500
MARK MANDEL: N1-standard-1.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.700
FRANCESC CAMPOY: N1-standard-1
or standard-1-N1.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.130
Where basically what
you're saying is,

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:51.390
I want one core
and a decent amount

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.780
of memory, not too
much, not too little.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:59.240
And then we also have with
two cores, four, eight, 16,

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:00.780
out to 32.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.280
And then you can
choose if you want

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.420
the more CPU or more memory.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:06.760
But that was pretty much it.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.620
So if you wanted,
for instance, to get

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:12.920
a machine that had 32
CPUs but you didn't really

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.450
want that much memory because
you were doing something that

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:21.000
was really, really computation
heavy but not that much memory,

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.024
you didn't really
have an option.

00:02:23.024 --> 00:02:24.690
MARK MANDEL: It's
kind of cookie cutter.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:26.520
You know, you've got a
one size fits all so--

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:26.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:27.340
MARK MANDEL: To a degree.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:28.131
More than one size.

00:02:28.131 --> 00:02:30.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Many very
different sizes but not enough.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:30.940
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.330
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So we've
basically fixed glitch.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:36.670
So we have it now, when
you create a new instance,

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.630
you actually have
in the menu a way

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:43.370
to choose between
either the default types

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:44.992
or you can create your own type.

00:02:44.992 --> 00:02:45.700
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:46.408
That sounds cool.

00:02:46.408 --> 00:02:49.030
So I can basically say,
this is how much CPU I want,

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:51.030
this is how much memory
I want, and I can custom

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:52.800
fit it basically to
whatever my needs are.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:53.120
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:53.620
Exactly.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:55.650
So you're going to
have two sliders,

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.700
one is going to be for
the number of CPUs--

00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:58.040
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:02:58.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
--the other one is

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:00.390
going to be for that
size of the memory.

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:01.190
That's it.

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:03.040
And you choose
whatever you need.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.260
MARK MANDEL: That
sounds really awesome.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:06.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And the best part

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.589
is the price of
[INAUDIBLE] to that.

00:03:07.589 --> 00:03:09.820
So if you're going to use
less memory, you pay less.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:10.120
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:10.470
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's great.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.150
MARK MANDEL: So also, if I
was running my own machines,

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:15.190
maybe on-prem or
something like that,

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:17.470
and I want to try and
match those in some way,

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.510
I could be, like, oh, now
I can built my machine

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:20.814
spec to match that exactly.

00:03:20.814 --> 00:03:21.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:22.229
Yeah.

00:03:22.229 --> 00:03:25.810
And also what I was thinking
is that you can also do tests

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:27.300
on it and see,
for instance, what

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.164
is the minimum amount of
memory that you need--

00:03:30.164 --> 00:03:31.080
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:31.820
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --on your app.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:33.070
So you can do that.

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:35.160
Just [INAUDIBLE], do
some tests, and see

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.260
how it behaves, and do it on
different kinds of missions

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:41.490
to actually be sure that the
instances that you're using

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.412
are the best suited for
what you want to do.

00:03:43.412 --> 00:03:44.120
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:44.520
OK.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:46.030
That sounds super,
super powerful.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:47.540
We've got a link
in the show notes,

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:49.706
see the blog post that
describes this, and obviously

00:03:49.706 --> 00:03:51.190
the documentation as well.

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:52.777
So that sounds really great.

00:03:52.777 --> 00:03:54.860
So why don't we get into
our interview with Chris.

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:56.110
It sounds like it's
going to be great too.

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:56.480
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:57.230
Let's go for that.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:58.210
MARK MANDEL: Brilliant.

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:00.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So we're
here with Chris Sells.

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:01.040
Hi, Chris.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.570
CHRIS SELLS: Hey, guys.

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:06.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And you
are a product manager here

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:11.950
at Google Cloud Platform
for development experience.

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:15.060
So tell us a little bit
about what does that mean.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:15.810
CHRIS SELLS: Sure.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:19.540
So what that means,
fundamentally,

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:27.100
is-- well, let's see,
let me say it this way.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:30.300
The old CEO at
Microsoft, he would

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.830
say-- he would leap
up on stage and say,

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:35.450
developers,
developers, developers.

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:36.780
That's what I do.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:40.555
I care deeply about making sure
that developers on the Google

00:04:40.555 --> 00:04:43.390
Cloud Platform have the
tools that they need

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:45.960
to be happy and successful.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:46.950
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:50.270
So developers, developers,
developers, and tools.

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:51.700
What are tools for you?

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:53.350
What are those tools
for developers?

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:54.170
CHRIS SELLS: Sure.

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:57.930
And in fact, that's actually
kind of an ongoing issue

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:01.890
because pretty much anything
that you can talk to

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:05.220
on our platform is a tool, from
the APIs, to the command lines,

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.071
to the web, to the
IDE extensions.

00:05:08.071 --> 00:05:08.570
Right?

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:10.403
I mean, you can think
of anything as a tool.

00:05:10.403 --> 00:05:14.330
But fundamentally, what
I care mostly about

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:21.180
is making sure that you have
a good way to write your code

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:22.530
to target our platform.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:27.170
So that's bring your own
editor, that's use Eclipse,

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:31.290
use Android Studio, use
other IDEs that we either

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:34.690
plug-in directly or
work with, partners

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:37.210
like JetBrains to make
sure it'll work well.

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:38.990
That's bringing
in libraries that

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:41.370
work really well for
your particular language

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:44.730
and program against not
just the cloud APIs but all

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:46.260
of the Google APIs.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:49.390
That's being able to
test your code locally,

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:52.560
make sure it runs the
way you would expect.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:55.540
That means being able
to check-in your code.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:58.420
That means being able
to package your code

00:05:58.420 --> 00:05:59.910
and deploy it to our platform.

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:03.260
And then once it's there,
being able to debug problems,

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:04.680
being able to look
at logs, being

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:08.460
able to just find
issues and fix them

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:11.460
and the cycle goes on and on.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:15.240
That range of tools,
through the whole set

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:19.200
of developer lifecycle
for a piece of software,

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:22.229
that's the set of
things that I push on.

00:06:22.229 --> 00:06:23.270
MARK MANDEL: So not much.

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:24.152
Not much at all.

00:06:24.152 --> 00:06:25.034
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:25.034 --> 00:06:25.920
Very little.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:26.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
It's a couple things.

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:28.130
MARK MANDEL: Couple things.

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:29.869
Not a very busy fellow really.

00:06:29.869 --> 00:06:31.660
CHRIS SELLS: Well, it's
funny you say that.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:33.000
But I feel pretty busy.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:33.750
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:35.750
That's-- I'm not surprising
by that [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:37.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Shocking.

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:40.722
So there's a lot of different
things going on in there.

00:06:40.722 --> 00:06:42.280
Can you talk a
little bit about what

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:44.670
are the last-- the
latest projects

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:45.810
you've been working on?

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:46.560
CHRIS SELLS: Sure.

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:51.640
So very proud, in
the last few months,

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:57.040
we have released
language specific

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:00.000
Getting Started pages for
the Google Cloud Platform

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.850
across four languages.

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:04.940
Up until now we've
been really good about,

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:09.680
hey, you want to learn about GCS
or Data Store, Cloud SQL or App

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:14.100
Engine or Compute Engine
from a product point of view.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:19.440
But a lot of developers kind of
consider themselves by language

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:20.090
first.

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:20.590
Right?

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:23.460
For example, they go to
Google and they type, Node,

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:26.149
how do I, Python,
how do I, right?

00:07:26.149 --> 00:07:26.815
I mean, that's--

00:07:26.815 --> 00:07:27.160
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:29.285
CHRIS SELLS: --how they
think of themselves, right?

00:07:29.285 --> 00:07:31.130
I'm a Java developer,
I'm a Ruby developer.

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:33.850
And so we weren't
doing a very good job

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:38.210
of kind of thinking about
the end and development story

00:07:38.210 --> 00:07:40.650
from how developers
identify themselves

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:41.860
from a specific language.

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:43.560
So what we've been
doing is thinking

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:46.650
about the set of
server side languages

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:50.560
that we really want to push
on and then making sure

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:53.930
that developers
have a place to go,

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:56.540
a home for that particular
language, on our platform.

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:58.340
And we've launched
four of those so far.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:06.030
So if you go to cloud.google.com
/nojs/python/go/ruby,

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:09.950
you will get our first four
language starting pages.

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:13.100
Entry points into our
platform for those languages.

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:14.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Thanks.

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:16.220
So I'm assuming
that includes how

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:18.325
to use the APIs
idiomatically and so on.

00:08:18.325 --> 00:08:19.200
CHRIS SELLS: It does.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:19.809
It does.

00:08:19.809 --> 00:08:21.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Does it also include

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:24.350
things like, for instance, if
you're a Python programmer you

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:26.790
might be using
Flask, on how to use

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:30.580
those very popular
libraries on our platform?

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:32.039
CHRIS SELLS: [COUGHING].

00:08:32.039 --> 00:08:34.970
Sorry.

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:37.014
That wasn't surprising,
I just had a cough.

00:08:37.014 --> 00:08:39.679
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:39.679 --> 00:08:43.559
So the answer is, for our
initial landing page--

00:08:43.559 --> 00:08:45.600
we've got, for all four
of these languages,

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:48.044
we've got two samples.

00:08:48.044 --> 00:08:49.960
Kind of a, Hello World,
what's the minimum you

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:52.650
need to do to bundle up
some code in that language

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:54.454
and get it running
on our platform.

00:08:54.454 --> 00:08:55.870
And then we've got
a bigger sample

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:58.180
which takes you
through a tour of some

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:01.030
of the technologies
around our platform.

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:02.595
And that's what
we have right now.

00:09:02.595 --> 00:09:04.720
And what we've done for
each one of these languages

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:06.940
is we've picked a
single framework.

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:09.402
For example, for
Ruby we picked Rails.

00:09:09.402 --> 00:09:11.110
Of course, Node is
built right in, right?

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:14.220
That's the framework
we use there.

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:15.400
And we've started there.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:18.420
Over time we have
all kinds of plans

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:21.670
to make sure we cover the
set of popular frameworks

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:25.140
and the set of popular tools
and, you know, I'm a Ruby-ist,

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:27.810
how do I do this and that
thing that I as a Ruby-ist

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:29.490
want to do and et cetera.

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:34.860
And we have big plans for
expanding that to cover those.

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:38.740
And then what I'd really love
to hear from your listeners

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:42.260
is what they would
like us to do.

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:45.060
What does a Ruby developer
want us to cover,

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:48.430
make sure that we have
instructions on how to do,

00:09:48.430 --> 00:09:50.350
or Python or et cetera.

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:53.130
So that's part of why
I'm here, to kind of let

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:56.310
folks know that there is
somebody at Google watching

00:09:56.310 --> 00:10:00.340
their backs as a developer from
all of this set of languages.

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:03.420
And if there is something
that you need from a tooling

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:07.110
point of view, I'm the
person you can reach out to,

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:08.980
and if I can't get
your question answered

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:10.700
or tell you where
we are on a feature,

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:14.071
then I can hook you
up with who can.

00:10:14.071 --> 00:10:14.820
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:15.440
Nice.

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:18.022
So you mentioned
that four languages

00:10:18.022 --> 00:10:19.230
got released on the Platform.

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:23.590
Of course, now I have to ask,
which one's your favorite?

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:26.540
CHRIS SELLS: Actually,
it's funny you ask that.

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:31.700
I, like every developer that's
ever picked up JavaScript,

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:36.190
have gone through my
five stages of grief.

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:36.690
Right?

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:38.260
I started at denial.

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:40.920
Oh, my Lord, I can't
believe somebody thinks this

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:42.410
is a real programming language.

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:45.360
And went through
anger and bargaining

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:47.140
and I went through
the whole thing,

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:50.840
and I eventually ended
up at acceptance.

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:55.490
Both from a client
programming point of view

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:57.940
and a server programming
point of view,

00:10:57.940 --> 00:10:59.870
of that set of
languages, I would

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:02.290
have to say that now
it is my favorite.

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:04.190
JavaScript as my favorite.

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:10.180
You have no idea how hard it is
for me to admit that in public.

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:12.611
Hello, my name is Chris
Sells and I like JavaScript.

00:11:12.611 --> 00:11:13.110
Right?

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:13.652
That's just--

00:11:13.652 --> 00:11:15.193
MARK MANDEL: That's
just [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:15.193 --> 00:11:16.220
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:18.428
CHRIS SELLS: And this is
after-- I mean, to be clear,

00:11:18.428 --> 00:11:23.410
my language history, I spent
probably 10 years in C,

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:27.070
10 years in C++-- there was
an overlap there, of course.

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:31.200
And then I gave all of that up
for C# and I did C# for more

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:32.310
than a decade probably.

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:36.070
I've done a bunch of C#
work in the Windows world.

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:38.780
And then right before
I came over to Google,

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:40.500
there was a period
of a few years where

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:44.960
I did a bunch of JavaScript work
as part of Windows 8 developer

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:46.840
experience for JavaScript.

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.160
And that's where I
kind of picked it up.

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:51.510
And at one point--
I don't know how

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:56.840
you guys are but I dream in
code sometimes-- and I woke up

00:11:56.840 --> 00:11:59.690
and I had to think, wait,
what language was that?

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:00.930
Because it was so natural.

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:03.170
I didn't know was it
JavaScript, was it C#?

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:06.450
And I knew that I had been
infected with JavaScript.

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:09.566
That's when I knew the
process was complete.

00:12:09.566 --> 00:12:11.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I like the
idea of talking of JavaScript

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:13.516
from an infective point of view.

00:12:13.516 --> 00:12:15.850
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:20.300
So you mentioned that you
tried to get feedback directly

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:21.310
from developers.

00:12:21.310 --> 00:12:22.980
One way of getting
that, of course,

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:26.210
is developers going to
us and talking to you,

00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:27.290
sending emails or--

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:27.590
CHRIS SELLS: Yes.

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:27.690
Yes.

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:28.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
[INAUDIBLE] bugs and so on.

00:12:28.490 --> 00:12:29.198
CHRIS SELLS: Yes.

00:12:29.198 --> 00:12:31.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What are
the other ways you find out

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:36.000
what had kind of features to add
or what kind of communication

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:37.014
to improve and so on?

00:12:37.014 --> 00:12:39.180
CHRIS SELLS: Actually,
that's a really good question

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:44.720
because the fundamental
job of a PM here at Google

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:49.750
is to understand the customer
needs, and in many cases,

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:52.760
to be the customer proxy.

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:54.790
To bring in customer
requirements

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:57.300
and represent them to
the engineering team

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:00.000
and convince them that
this is a good way to go.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.470
Because none of the engineers
work for me at Google.

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:04.160
I mean, it's not
like at Microsoft

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:07.740
where the PM got to say,
jump, and the engineers said,

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:08.240
how high?

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:10.240
That's not how it
works at Google at all.

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:13.220
It's very much
engineering centric.

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:18.050
So it's my job to have data.

00:13:18.050 --> 00:13:22.820
And that data comes in the
form of-- we do surveys a lot,

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:26.850
we do something we call
critical user journeys where

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:30.690
we kind of layout an end
to end scenario and have

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:34.130
our customers with our
user experience researchers

00:13:34.130 --> 00:13:36.330
work their way through
these scenarios

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:37.930
and see how well they do.

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:43.480
And we take both completion
metrics, which is quantitative,

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:46.440
and comments-- verbatims-- along
the way, which is qualitative.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:49.100
So we lean on that
kind of data a lot.

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:52.630
And then we lean on
customer sentiment as well.

00:13:52.630 --> 00:13:55.370
We get to see what people
are doing on Stack Overflow,

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:57.330
we get to see what people
are doing on Twitter,

00:13:57.330 --> 00:14:00.030
we get to see what people are
doing on our Google Groups.

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:03.960
And all of that forms
the basis of, really,

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:07.420
a ton of data that
we've got that we

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:09.997
use to inform our decisions
about what to build

00:14:09.997 --> 00:14:11.830
and when to build it
and how important it is

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:14.576
and how to focus our
product planning.

00:14:14.576 --> 00:14:15.700
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very nice.

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:17.540
MARK MANDEL: Very nice, indeed.

00:14:17.540 --> 00:14:19.850
You mentioned
previously, you talked

00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:23.360
about IDEs and some integration
and some partnerships.

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:26.420
Personally, I'm a huge
IntelliJ fan, I know that.

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:28.170
But can you tell us
some more about what's

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:30.975
happening in the IDE space,
maybe specifically IntelliJ

00:14:30.975 --> 00:14:33.314
because I'm watching
that a little bit.

00:14:33.314 --> 00:14:34.230
I'd love to hear more.

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:35.900
I'm sure there's a
broader spectrum of people

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:36.720
other than just
what I specifically

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:37.530
want to hear about.

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:38.780
CHRIS SELLS: Well, absolutely.

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:43.060
So we have-- we shipped at
the beginning of this year

00:14:43.060 --> 00:14:45.370
an extension to
Android Studio called

00:14:45.370 --> 00:14:47.451
Cloud Tools for Android Studio.

00:14:47.451 --> 00:14:49.450
Although it's bundled
right into Android Studio,

00:14:49.450 --> 00:14:50.800
you don't have to
install it separately,

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:52.008
it just comes out of the box.

00:14:52.008 --> 00:14:55.030
But essentially,
what it enables is

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:58.000
you to be a full stack developer
inside of Android Studio.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:14:59.750
You can build your
Android client,

00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:03.550
you can build your
Java based GCP backend,

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:06.350
and we'll even build
a client library.

00:15:06.350 --> 00:15:10.760
And as you change your backend,
we'll update the client library

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:14.100
and make sure that
if there's a change

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:15.940
then you don't get
a compiler error

00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:20.490
and you get state incompletion
and things like that.

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:22.220
So we built that
into Android Studio

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:25.700
and that's been shipping
all of this year.

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:28.360
And we've found that
to be very popular.

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:31.840
But as you know, or maybe
you don't, the Android Studio

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:36.150
is in fact a fork of IntelliJ
Community Edition, the Open

00:15:36.150 --> 00:15:36.650
Source.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:37.130
MARK MANDEL: I knew it.

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:37.880
CHRIS SELLS: Yeah.

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:39.900
So I figured you would.

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:42.330
And so, as you
might imagine, it's

00:15:42.330 --> 00:15:46.200
not very far for
us to go from, hey,

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:50.130
let's extend Android Studio,
to let's extend IntelliJ

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:51.890
all together.

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:56.880
And so, in fact, we
have a GitHub repository

00:15:56.880 --> 00:16:02.250
with the source code of a
Cloud Tools for IntelliJ

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:03.880
that's in the works.

00:16:03.880 --> 00:16:06.650
It's not as full-featured yet
as the Cloud Tools for Android

00:16:06.650 --> 00:16:08.350
Studio but we're
working on it and we

00:16:08.350 --> 00:16:12.650
hope to have that not only
updated in the repository

00:16:12.650 --> 00:16:15.560
but to have a
binary distribution

00:16:15.560 --> 00:16:18.520
by the end of the year so you
can actually deploy from inside

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:21.310
of IntelliJ to our platform.

00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:26.200
And right now what the
IntelliJ plug-in does,

00:16:26.200 --> 00:16:29.700
it actually attaches
to Cloud Debugger.

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:32.080
I don't know if you guys know
how Cloud Debugger works,

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:34.830
but for your listeners, the
way the Cloud Debugger works

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:39.967
is you have your running
Java application,

00:16:39.967 --> 00:16:41.800
for example-- we're
talking about Java now--

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:44.820
but you have your running Java
application on our platform,

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:47.730
and there's some
hard to find bug that

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:50.320
only happens in production.

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:52.554
You can't reproduce it on
your development machine,

00:16:52.554 --> 00:16:53.970
you can't reproduce
it internally,

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:55.460
these are the ones
that kill you.

00:16:55.460 --> 00:17:00.220
That now you're adding-- you're
inserting printf statements

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:03.590
every other line to figure
out what's going on.

00:17:03.590 --> 00:17:07.790
And so what we do in
the Cloud Debugger

00:17:07.790 --> 00:17:13.390
is you can actually pull up the
source code for the application

00:17:13.390 --> 00:17:17.730
in production and set what is
the equivalent of a breakpoint,

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:19.839
except we don't actually break.

00:17:19.839 --> 00:17:21.530
We never stop the process.

00:17:21.530 --> 00:17:24.680
We don't-- in fact, the
user can't even tell.

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:28.349
We make sure that we don't
spend more than 10 milliseconds

00:17:28.349 --> 00:17:30.730
grabbing data-- and
that's the call stack

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:36.090
and that's the locals-- so
that you can take that data

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:40.430
and dig into it as much as
you want to without harming

00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:41.340
the user experience.

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:43.790
And you can track down what
the problem is that only

00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:45.240
happens in production.

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:49.700
Now, that works all great on the
cloud, but for that to happen,

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:52.060
of course, you need to
give us your source code.

00:17:52.060 --> 00:17:54.456
So in a lot of cases, we
find that our customers are

00:17:54.456 --> 00:17:56.580
enterprise customers and
they don't have permission

00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:59.410
to give us our source code,
right-- their source code.

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:01.440
So the nice thing about
the IntelliJ plug-in

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:03.500
is it works against
the source code you've

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:05.560
got on your local
machine but it never

00:18:05.560 --> 00:18:08.060
makes it to the Google Cloud.

00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:10.950
So you can do all of your
debugging for the Cloud

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:13.340
Debugger inside of
IntelliJ without giving us

00:18:13.340 --> 00:18:15.110
your source code.

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:16.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:16.510
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:17.400
It's very cool.

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:21.280
That's a really fine point
I didn't actually realize.

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:21.780
Wow.

00:18:21.780 --> 00:18:21.930
OK.

00:18:21.930 --> 00:18:22.220
Cool.

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:23.928
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
very interesting.

00:18:23.928 --> 00:18:26.680
So I have another
question regarding IDEs.

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:31.000
I'm a Go developer-- I
do Go most of the time--

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:35.070
and I was pretty excited to
hear that there was for--

00:18:35.070 --> 00:18:37.800
and I actually, you used to work
at Microsoft so you may know

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:41.280
a lot about that-- so Visual
Studio-- Visual Studio

00:18:41.280 --> 00:18:43.322
code-- has been open sourced.

00:18:43.322 --> 00:18:44.030
CHRIS SELLS: Yes.

00:18:44.030 --> 00:18:46.029
FRANCESC CAMPOY: This
is a very interesting--

00:18:46.029 --> 00:18:47.070
very interesting project.

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:49.403
And I know that, for instance,
they added support for Go

00:18:49.403 --> 00:18:51.880
with debuggers and so on.

00:18:51.880 --> 00:18:55.500
Like, probably they announced it
last week, something like that.

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:59.350
Is there any effort
that we are working--

00:18:59.350 --> 00:19:03.170
we are working to have support
for Google Cloud Platform on VS

00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:03.670
code?

00:19:03.670 --> 00:19:08.100
And if not, is that something
that our community could do

00:19:08.100 --> 00:19:09.656
or how does that work?

00:19:09.656 --> 00:19:11.030
CHRIS SELLS: So
it's interesting.

00:19:11.030 --> 00:19:14.040
So let's back up and think
about the technology involved.

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:17.670
One piece of technology
is, of course--

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:20.500
if we think about that
end to end developers

00:19:20.500 --> 00:19:23.430
set of scenarios that I
care about, one of them

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:26.440
is having a set of
idiomatic client libraries.

00:19:26.440 --> 00:19:28.540
Client libraries that
really work well.

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:32.830
And we already have-- there's
something like 267 Google

00:19:32.830 --> 00:19:37.570
APIs-- we already have NuGet
libraries to cover the APIs

00:19:37.570 --> 00:19:42.930
and we have a great deal, very
non-trivial amount of usage,

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:47.717
through .NET clients today using
Google APIs and Google Cloud

00:19:47.717 --> 00:19:48.880
APIs.

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:50.927
So that's already one
part of the story.

00:19:50.927 --> 00:19:53.010
You can go to NuGet, you
can grab those libraries,

00:19:53.010 --> 00:19:54.720
you can bring them in
your project today.

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:56.540
OK?

00:19:56.540 --> 00:20:01.270
We also have the fact that we
have the Compute Engine that

00:20:01.270 --> 00:20:02.170
runs Windows Server.

00:20:02.170 --> 00:20:04.440
Of course you could
imagine deploying

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:07.079
you know your ASP.NET app to
that Windows Server, right?

00:20:07.079 --> 00:20:08.620
It's built right
into Windows Server.

00:20:08.620 --> 00:20:13.000
There's nothing to stop you
from doing that kind of thing.

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:16.890
And of course we also have the
cloud SDK that, in addition

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:18.720
to working on Linux
and Mac, of course

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:22.510
we have a Windows Installer
and you can install and use

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:23.030
that today.

00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:29.880
So we already do quite a bit for
the .NET developer on Windows.

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:33.220
So getting back
to your question,

00:20:33.220 --> 00:20:36.546
are we going to
dig into VS code?

00:20:36.546 --> 00:20:37.920
That's actually--
let me ask that

00:20:37.920 --> 00:20:39.170
as a question to the audience.

00:20:39.170 --> 00:20:43.100
Because VS code is interesting
because it is essentially

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:47.030
kind of Microsoft's
lightweight IDE-less IDE.

00:20:47.030 --> 00:20:50.320
It's kind of like subline
text with debugging and built

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:51.970
in support for C#.

00:20:51.970 --> 00:20:53.800
That's a good way
to think about it.

00:20:53.800 --> 00:20:57.500
It is not at all like
the heavyweight IDE

00:20:57.500 --> 00:21:00.420
that Visual Studio
is on Windows.

00:21:00.420 --> 00:21:03.890
And the question is, how
important is that integration

00:21:03.890 --> 00:21:05.150
to our audience?

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:08.840
Is that something that they
think is high on their list?

00:21:08.840 --> 00:21:13.900
Do they want to do C# .NET based
development on a Mac, on Linux?

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:18.580
Or do they want to fire it up
on Windows using the full Visual

00:21:18.580 --> 00:21:19.346
Studio desktop?

00:21:21.860 --> 00:21:25.470
Right now it seems pretty clear
that the usage is Visual Studio

00:21:25.470 --> 00:21:29.070
desktop, but I'm curious
what the adoption will be--

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:32.290
and of course, based on that
then, how quickly we should

00:21:32.290 --> 00:21:35.084
think about supporting it.

00:21:35.084 --> 00:21:37.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's a very
good question that I cannot

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:38.304
really answer.

00:21:38.304 --> 00:21:38.970
MARK MANDEL: No.

00:21:38.970 --> 00:21:40.740
And then again, like
you were saying,

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:42.390
you want to reach
to the audience

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:44.470
and we'll definitely put
all your contact details

00:21:44.470 --> 00:21:47.160
in the show notes so that you
can have those conversations

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:49.350
and we can find
those things out.

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:51.570
Because that's important
information to have.

00:21:51.570 --> 00:21:52.090
CHRIS SELLS: Absolutely.

00:21:52.090 --> 00:21:54.200
And this is part of how
I gather that data to be

00:21:54.200 --> 00:21:56.540
able to make product decisions.

00:21:56.540 --> 00:21:58.380
MARK MANDEL: So that
sounds really cool.

00:21:58.380 --> 00:22:03.010
So now I'm curious
whether-- so I

00:22:03.010 --> 00:22:05.150
know there is new and
interesting stuff coming up

00:22:05.150 --> 00:22:06.010
in the roadmap.

00:22:06.010 --> 00:22:09.160
I'm wondering if you're
able to share anything

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:12.630
or at least tease a little
on what sort of cool things

00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:13.842
might be coming.

00:22:13.842 --> 00:22:15.050
I know we all work at Google.

00:22:15.050 --> 00:22:15.820
I feel terrible asking.

00:22:15.820 --> 00:22:17.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool
but not confidential.

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:17.340
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:18.881
Cool but no confidential
thinks we're

00:22:18.881 --> 00:22:20.720
going to get in trouble for.

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:21.220
Yeah.

