00:30:16.430 --> 00:30:20.390
The question of the week today,
it's related to Kubernetes

00:30:20.390 --> 00:30:24.300
So we talked about it
with-- in episode number two

00:30:24.300 --> 00:30:27.930
with Brain Dorsey-- and one of
the things that we discussed

00:30:27.930 --> 00:30:30.300
was [INAUDIBLE]
controllers which basically

00:30:30.300 --> 00:30:33.050
allows you to say
how many instances

00:30:33.050 --> 00:30:35.270
or how many replicas
of a given bot

00:30:35.270 --> 00:30:37.370
or set of containers
you want to have.

00:30:37.370 --> 00:30:39.590
And the question
that we received

00:30:39.590 --> 00:30:45.470
was, well, yeah, that's fine,
but does it also autoscale?

00:30:45.470 --> 00:30:48.670
Like, will it create
more instances

00:30:48.670 --> 00:30:51.310
if I have more traffic or
any other requirements?

00:30:51.310 --> 00:30:53.004
So do you know that?

00:30:53.004 --> 00:30:54.170
MARK MANDEL: I do know this.

00:30:54.170 --> 00:30:54.370
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Wonderful.

00:30:54.370 --> 00:30:56.953
MARK MANDEL: This is a question
I get quite regularly whenever

00:30:56.953 --> 00:30:58.670
I present about Kubernetes.

00:30:58.670 --> 00:31:01.300
So the really cool thing is
is several weeks ago-- not

00:31:01.300 --> 00:31:04.710
too long ago now--
Kubernetes 1.1 was released,

00:31:04.710 --> 00:31:06.990
and one of the great
new features about that

00:31:06.990 --> 00:31:08.960
is horizontal pod order scaling.

00:31:08.960 --> 00:31:10.580
Which is awesome.

00:31:10.580 --> 00:31:13.440
It does literally exactly
what you would describe.

00:31:13.440 --> 00:31:15.960
You're able to configure it
such that to create and decrease

00:31:15.960 --> 00:31:17.418
the number of pods
that are running

00:31:17.418 --> 00:31:19.410
or the number of containers
that are running.

00:31:19.410 --> 00:31:21.427
The way it works is
through CPU order scaling.

00:31:21.427 --> 00:31:22.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:24.384
MARK MANDEL: So the number--
I'm going to make sure

00:31:24.384 --> 00:31:27.280
I read this right because
it's mathematical.

00:31:27.280 --> 00:31:30.340
The CPU utilization is
the recent CPU usage

00:31:30.340 --> 00:31:33.260
of a pod divided by the
sum of CPU requested

00:31:33.260 --> 00:31:34.785
by the pod's containers.

00:31:34.785 --> 00:31:35.660
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Wow.

00:31:35.660 --> 00:31:35.950
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:31:35.950 --> 00:31:36.910
I mad sure I read that right.

00:31:36.910 --> 00:31:38.430
So basically, the
short gist of it

00:31:38.430 --> 00:31:40.060
is, is you say
things like, I want

00:31:40.060 --> 00:31:42.590
a minimum number of
replicas-- say, one--

00:31:42.590 --> 00:31:45.230
I want a maximum number
of replicas-- so, 10--

00:31:45.230 --> 00:31:46.790
and then you say, OK.

00:31:46.790 --> 00:31:49.020
The target percentage
is, say, 50,

00:31:49.020 --> 00:31:51.510
so you want to make
sure that the CPU

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:53.710
usage stays under that 50%.

00:31:53.710 --> 00:31:55.140
And so if you start
going over it,

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:56.806
it will start creating
pods to make sure

00:31:56.806 --> 00:31:58.070
that CPU usage comes down.

00:31:58.070 --> 00:31:59.280
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I see.

00:31:59.280 --> 00:32:00.720
MARK MANDEL: So you're
able to do cool stuff so

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:02.380
that you can load
test application,

00:32:02.380 --> 00:32:04.672
make sure that CPU
usage is right,

00:32:04.672 --> 00:32:06.630
you can create the number
of pods that you need

00:32:06.630 --> 00:32:08.480
and then you can say,
OK, can we handle

00:32:08.480 --> 00:32:10.900
a million requests per
second or whatever it is you

00:32:10.900 --> 00:32:12.644
might actually have to need.

00:32:12.644 --> 00:32:13.810
It's pretty straightforward.

00:32:13.810 --> 00:32:15.550
And again, it's
through a YAML file,

00:32:15.550 --> 00:32:17.302
very standard stuff
is Kubernetes.

00:32:17.302 --> 00:32:19.010
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
very interesting.

00:32:19.010 --> 00:32:21.000
So that's something
quite new you said?

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:21.791
MARK MANDEL: It is.

00:32:21.791 --> 00:32:23.250
It came in the 1.1 release.

00:32:23.250 --> 00:32:27.150
It's part of the extensions
beta type stuff, which is still

00:32:27.150 --> 00:32:28.270
in Invader in Kubernetes.

00:32:28.270 --> 00:32:30.250
But definitely worth
playing with it.

00:32:30.250 --> 00:32:31.670
It's going to be a very
powerful new feature.

00:32:31.670 --> 00:32:33.086
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Very interesting.

00:32:33.086 --> 00:32:35.040
